Glacial till areas
Soil properties for one of the hydrogeological environments described in Lundmark, A., 
2008. Monitoring transport and fate of de-icing salt in the roadside environment - Modelling 
and field measurements. Doctoral thesis in Land and Water Resources Engineering, 
TRITA-LWR PHD 1038. KTH Architecture and the Built Environment.

Conceptual description:
The till is very heterogeneous both with depth and across the landscape. The 
composition is mainly sandy-silty and the thickness is usually less than 4-5 m, except for 
depressions where the thickness can be larger. The groundwater level is generally close 
to the ground surface due to low hydraulic conductivity. Typical depth to groundwater is 
1-3 m, with an annual fluctuation that can be a couple of meters. The soil is mostly 
overgrown by coniferous forest, which is assumed to be dominated by spruce. 

Soil profile:
Glacial till 0-4 m depth
Bedrock >4 m depth

Brooks and Corey parameters for till represent effective parameters 
reported in Lundmark & Jansson: Generic soil descriptions for modelling water and chloride
dynamics in the unsaturated zone based on Swedish soils. Submitted December 2007 to
Geoderma. 

2008-03-17 Annika Lundmark 
 